---
id: compose-01
courseId: frontend-architecture
title: "Ways to ==compose== a frontend"
concepts: [composition-techniques, composition-requirements, module-federation-place]
---

{% hook id="h1" estSeconds=40 character="bug" concepts=["composition-techniques"] %}
Monday stand-up. Three teams, one question: how do we put our parts on one page? Team A said npm packages. Team B said iframes. Team C said ==Module Federation==, obviously. All three said "microfrontends". I stayed quiet and ate the pizza.
{% margin %}
{% stop %}
Same goal, three different tools. What would you ask before picking one?
{% /stop %}
{% /margin %}
{% /hook %}

{% predict id="p1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/runtime-import.txt" language="js" concepts=["composition-techniques", "module-federation-place"] %}
{% option output="cart v2" correct=true %}
import() takes an address that is only known while the code runs. The cart part was never in a build. Source: [MDN, import()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/import); [Node.js, ESM data: imports](https://nodejs.org/api/esm.html#data-imports).
{% /option %}
{% option output="Error: Cannot find module" misconception="mf-means-module-federation" %}
A plain import() can already load a module from an address chosen at run time. A federation plugin adds a manifest, shared dependencies and a runtime on top of that idea; it is not the only way to load code later.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So the host doesn't need to know the part at build time?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e1" estSeconds=50 code="./samples/join-moments.txt" concepts=["composition-techniques"] %}
Every technique joins separately built parts at one of three moments. Module Federation is one of four browser-side options, not the definition of microfrontends. Webpack describes it as [multiple separate builds forming a single application](https://webpack.js.org/concepts/module-federation/), "often known as Micro-Frontends, but is not limited to that". Primary docs:

- [nginx server-side includes](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_ssi_module.html)
- [MDN iframe](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/iframe), [import maps](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/script/type/importmap), [web components](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components)

{% annotation line=1 %}
Code lives inside your build.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=2 %}
Assembled before the response leaves.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=6 %}
One of four browser-side options.
{% /annotation %}
{% margin %}
{% gotcha %}
Other places to compose exist; this lesson covers only the server example in the nginx docs.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /explain %}

{% predict id="p2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/ssi-include.http" language="http" concepts=["composition-techniques"] %}
{% option output="<h1>Mug</h1><p>Cart: 2 items</p>" correct=true %}
nginx's SSI module is a filter that processes include commands in responses passing through it, and an include puts the result of another request into the response. The browser gets one assembled page. Source: [nginx, ngx_http_ssi_module](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_ssi_module.html).
{% /option %}
{% option output="<h1>Mug</h1><!--# include virtual=\"/fragments/cart\" -->" misconception="mf-means-module-federation" %}
That would happen with ssi off, which is the default. With it on, the include is resolved on the server. A browser would only see an HTML comment and fetch nothing.
{% /option %}
{% option output="<h1>Mug</h1><iframe src=\"/fragments/cart\"></iframe>" misconception="one-technique-fits-all" %}
A server include is not an iframe. It pastes the fragment's HTML into the page before the response is sent, so the browser sees one document.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% fillBlanks id="f1" estSeconds=45 template="<!--# block name=\"cart-stub\" -->\n<p>Cart unavailable</p>\n<!--# endblock -->\n<!--# include virtual=\"/fragments/cart\" ___param___=\"cart-stub\" -->" language="http" concepts=["composition-techniques"] %}
{% blank id="param" accepted=["stub"] %}
The stub parameter names the block that is shown if the included request gives an empty body or fails. The nginx docs call it a non-standard SSI parameter. Source: [nginx, ngx_http_ssi_module](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_ssi_module.html).
{% /blank %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="says" %}
Oh, so one broken fragment doesn't have to break the whole page.
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% fillBlanks id="f2" estSeconds=45 template="<script type=\"___type___\">\n  { \"imports\": { \"___spec___\": \"https://cdn.example.com/cart/index.js\" } }\n</script>\n<script type=\"module\">\n  import { render } from \"cart\";\n</script>" language="http" concepts=["composition-techniques"] %}
{% blank id="type" accepted=["importmap"] %}
An import map is declared with a script element of type importmap. It must come before the scripts that use it. Source: [MDN, script type importmap](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/script/type/importmap).
{% /blank %}
{% blank id="spec" accepted=["cart"] %}
The key is the bare module specifier written in the import statement. The browser replaces it with the mapped address when it resolves the import. Source: [MDN, script type importmap](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/script/type/importmap).
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% fillBlanks id="f3" estSeconds=45 template="class CartWidget extends HTMLElement {}\ncustomElements.___method___(\"cart-widget\", CartWidget);" language="js" concepts=["composition-techniques"] %}
{% blank id="method" accepted=["define"] %}
You implement a class, then register it with customElements.define. After that the browser knows the tag. Shadow DOM is a separate, optional step that gives the element a private tree. Source: [MDN, Using custom elements](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components/Using_custom_elements).
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p3" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/iframe.http" language="http" concepts=["composition-techniques", "composition-requirements"] %}
{% option output="A nested browsing context with its own document, which costs extra memory and compute" correct=true %}
An iframe is a nested browsing context that embeds another document. Each one is a complete document environment, so each needs more memory and other computing resources. Source: [MDN, iframe](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/iframe).
{% /option %}
{% option output="Cart elements added to the host page's own document" misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
No. The cart stays in a different document. That separation is what you get from an iframe, and it is also what you pay for.
{% /option %}
{% option output="A script that adds the cart's code to the host's module graph" misconception="mf-means-module-federation" %}
No. An iframe loads a whole page, not a module. Only techniques such as import maps or Module Federation load modules into the page's own module graph.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So isolation is bought with memory?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e2" estSeconds=50 code="./samples/host-remotes.txt" concepts=["module-federation-place"] %}
Module Federation 2.0 [adds a manifest, a runtime and a plugin system](https://module-federation.io/guide/start/index.html) to the idea from webpack. A remote [publishes mf-manifest.json](https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/manifest-snapshot.html), which lists what it exposes and where its entry is. The host only names the remotes. This is the host config of this course's example project.

{% annotation line=3 %}
Each remote has its own address.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=4 %}
Address of a manifest, not of code.
{% /annotation %}
{% margin %}
{% gotcha %}
In the recorded run of the example, the host page showed the headings Remote a and Remote b. Next lesson shows how they got there.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /explain %}

{% fillBlanks id="f4" estSeconds=45 template="remotes: {\n  remote_a: 'remote_a@http://localhost:3001/___file___',\n}" language="ts" concepts=["module-federation-place"] %}
{% blank id="file" accepted=["mf-manifest.json"] %}
Remotes in the Module Federation docs and in the example project point at mf-manifest.json. Source: [Module Federation, Rsbuild integration](https://module-federation.io/integrations/build-tool/rsbuild.html).
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p4" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/shared-state.txt" language="js" concepts=["composition-requirements"] %}
{% option output="3 2" correct=true %}
Each build has its own private copy of the module, so each copy counts on its own: 3 calls on the host copy, 2 on the remote copy. Webpack's shared modules exist to avoid this: they come from one share scope instead of a private copy. Source: [webpack, Module Federation](https://webpack.js.org/concepts/module-federation/).
{% /option %}
{% option output="3 3" misconception="shared-is-automatic" %}
Two copies do not share a counter just because they are the same code. Sharing has to be set up, and you will break it on purpose in lesson 4.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% stop %}
Which techniques put both sides on a single copy of a module?
{% /stop %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% predict id="p5" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/rebuild.txt" language="js" concepts=["composition-requirements"] %}
{% option output="npm packages" correct=true %}
A package is a local module, part of the current build, so a new version needs a new build of the host. A server include is resolved on each request. An import map and Module Federation load parts in the browser. Source: [webpack, Module Federation](https://webpack.js.org/concepts/module-federation/).
{% /option %}
{% option output="npm packages, server includes, import map" misconception="mf-means-module-federation" %}
Module Federation is not the only way to avoid a rebuild. Server includes and import maps also pick up a new part without rebuilding the host's code.
{% /option %}
{% option output="(empty line)" misconception="one-technique-fits-all" %}
Techniques differ on independent deploy. Build-time packages tie the part to the host's build.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% pitfall id="pf1" estSeconds=45 language="js" badCode="./samples/decision-bad.txt" goodCode="./samples/decision-good.txt" concepts=["composition-requirements"] %}
Start from the requirements, not from the tool you know. Our opinion, not a rule: a shell that composes parts in the browser fits an app behind a login. Crawlers that skip JavaScript need server-made HTML: [Google](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/javascript/javascript-seo-basics) still calls server-side rendering "a great idea". [Module Federation SSR](https://module-federation.io/integrations/framework/modernjs/index.html) exists in the Modern.js integration.
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Product pages looked empty to the crawler. Excellent. I love a quiet crawler.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /pitfall %}

{% predict id="p6" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/crawler.http" language="http" concepts=["composition-requirements"] %}
{% option output="Nothing from the parts: the root div is empty until shell.js runs" correct=true %}
Without JavaScript the response has no product content. Googlebot does render JavaScript, in a queue that can take longer, and Google notes that not all bots can. Source: [Google, JavaScript SEO basics](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/javascript/javascript-seo-basics).
{% /option %}
{% option output="The full product page, because the parts are built into the shell" misconception="one-technique-fits-all" %}
The parts are loaded by shell.js in the browser. Whether a technique gives you SEO depends on where it joins the parts, not on the word microfrontends.
{% /option %}
{% option output="The full product page, because Module Federation renders on the server" misconception="mf-means-module-federation" %}
Module Federation can be used with SSR in the Modern.js integration, but that is a setup you choose. This response comes from a shell that does not do it. Source: [Module Federation, Modern.js](https://module-federation.io/integrations/framework/modernjs/index.html).
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% recap id="rc1" estSeconds=30 concepts=["composition-techniques", "composition-requirements", "module-federation-place"] %}
{% point %}
Parts are joined at **build time**, **request time** or **browser run time**.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
Each requirement rules some techniques out: independent deploy, shared state, style isolation, SEO.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
Module Federation is **one browser-side option**: separate builds, loaded through a manifest.
{% /point %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Pick one tool for everything. I'll handle the incidents.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /recap %}

{% predict id="p7" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/failing-part.txt" language="js" concepts=["composition-requirements"] %}
{% option output="host survived: cart is down" correct=true %}
The part is loaded while the host runs. If evaluating it throws, import() rejects with the thrown error, and try and catch can handle it. Source: [MDN, import()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/import).
{% /option %}
{% option output="Build failed before anything ran" misconception="one-technique-fits-all" %}
There is no build step that could fail for this part, because it was never part of the build. That is the price of joining at run time: failures show up while users are on the page.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% cliffhanger id="c1" estSeconds=20 nextLessonId="host-remote-01" question="Module Federation loads parts in the browser. Who is the host, who is the remote, and which one asks the network for the other?" %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Next lesson I'm hiding in a request. Find me before it loads.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /cliffhanger %}
